# WordPress scheduled jobs, every minute.
#
# Installed to /etc/cron.d/wp-cron, which is why each line carries a user field. Debian's cron
# ignores a file in that directory if it is group- or world-writable, or if its name contains a
# dot — see the Dockerfile COPY, which lands it as `wp-cron` with mode 0644.
#
# Every task in the service runs this. That is intentional and safe rather than something to
# engineer around: wp-cron.php takes WordPress's `doing_cron` lock, so of N tasks firing in the
# same minute exactly one runs the due jobs and the rest return immediately. The cost is N-1
# loopback requests a minute, which is nothing; the benefit is that no single task is special,
# so a rolling deploy or a failed task never takes scheduled work down with it.
#
# A trailing newline is required — cron silently skips a final line without one.
* * * * * root /usr/local/bin/wp-cron.sh
